# Files Docker leaves out of the build context.
#
# The build context is the folder of files the Docker builder may read: the
# `.` in `docker build -t portfolio .`, and `context: .` in the CI workflow.
# COPY can only copy files from the context, and files listed here are
# removed from it, as if they didn't exist.
#
# The Dockerfile copies just what the build needs (package.json and the lock
# file, vite.config.ts, tsconfig.json, scripts/, site/, nginx.conf), so this
# list isn't what keeps the image small today. It's a safety net: if a
# `COPY . .` is ever added, none of the files below can slip into the image,
# and they're never sent to the builder (which may be a remote machine).
#
# Rules: one pattern per line, matched from the root of the context; a
# comment line must start with # in the very first column.
#
# Try it: docker build --progress=plain -t portfolio .
#   The "load build context" step is the context being sent to the builder.
# Learn more: https://docs.docker.com/build/concepts/context/

# Git history and CI config: not part of the site.
.git
.github
# The Kubernetes manifests are deployed by Flux, not baked into the image.
k8s
# Tests run in CI's test job, before the image is built.
tests
# The image runs its own `npm ci` on Linux. A local node_modules can hold
# native binaries built for another system, and it's large.
node_modules
# Build output: the image builds it fresh, so a stale local copy must never
# be mixed in.
dist
dist-ssr
# README.md and HANDOFF.md. `*.md` only matches at the root of the context;
# `**/*.md` would match in every folder.
*.md
